e  future  through a vaccination program uh  in the same way that we do flu each year  um  we have annual vaccines then do you  think like flu i i think it's highly  likely that there'll be a dual  vaccination program  uh you know for the foreseeable and this  is in the medium  the medium term um of flu and  covid you know flu vaccination rates are  at their highest level ever  we've over 80 percent of the over 65  year olds have  been vaccinated for flu this year that's  the biggest increases big jump on last  year when it was around 70  that's that's very good news it's good  news for two reasons firstly  to protect people against flu secondly  because it shows the vast vast majority  of over 65s  are up for getting vaccinated which is  brilliant and  important um so for the foreseeable  future i  i expect that we'll have a dual flu and  covert vaccination program  not least because we've seen these new  variants so far there's no evidence that  any new variant  uh it gets around the vaccine  but we'll have to update the vaccines to  be targeted  if further new variants come through  okay um  just quickly before we move on yes or no  it's fine  have you spoken to tony blair about  vaccinations well i talk to all sorts of  people all the time  and uh what about tony blair say yes  well i talk  i'm not gonna go into private  conversations i talk to all sorts of  people and  we take uh ideas from from from lots of  sources  okay not a no um now school's really  important of course for so many  of our viewers and children and parents  schools are only  open to the vulnerable and  the children of key workers but of  course we know that it does feel as  though schools are a lot busier than  they were during the first lockdown  if you're a key worker and you could  educate your children from home should  you avoid sending them to school if  possible  yes it's always been the guidance that  schools are  there for key workers children where  key workers need to have the children in  school in order to be able to get  to work so say one of you is a key  worker then do you think that really you  should be keeping your child at home  if at all possible yes so for instance  if you're a key worker  and you're and your partner and doesn't  work  then you shouldn't be sending your  children to school that's clear in the  guidance  um but of course the reason that we keep  schools open  for key workers children is that this is  important you know it's important that  uh that key workers for instance in the  nhs but not just the nhs can get to work  and and so it's a very difficult balance  to strike  i understand that more people are  sending their children to school than  they did  uh last time we've also got but we we  really do need  everybody who works in the nhs where  it's all possible to be able to  make it to work because you know these  pressures are real and significant and  you know i just want to um thank all the  people who are going out to work in the  nhs  even under these pressure circumstances  i'm i'm constantly inspired by the  amazing work ethic of everybody in the  nhs they yeah some people  go above and beyond something we can  certainly all get behind um  on schools within the last week the  prime minister has said  that schools are safe and that schools  may act as vectors  a transmission of the virus how can both  of those statements be true  well both are true and they're not they  can't both be true can they i mean look  you can say that  schools are low risk that the benefits  of going to school outweigh the risks  of losing out on the education but i  just can't see how you can say that  schools  are safe but they also transmit the  virus how does that work  well then let me explain because the  prime minister i thought explained it  extremely well  both last sunday and last monday when  this was being discussed  schools are safe for children the risk  to children is extremely low  and there's no evidence of more teachers  catching covid than any other profession  so that means schools are safe but the  challenge is that because children often  catch it asymptomatically  i mean adults do too right a third of  adults  who get coveted don't have any symptoms  at all but still pass it on  with children that proportion is much  much higher and so  the the so schools can still uh  cause spread whilst being safe for the  children who are in them  so that's that's the precise explanation  for why the prime minister was right to  say schools are both  safe but they do contribute to the  spread which is why unfortunately we've  had to close them  you can see why you know some teachers  are holding their heads in their hands  you know on sunday they're told schools  are safe on monday they're ordered to  close  gavin williamson is one week threatening  councils with legal action if they close  schools then he's later doing it himself  the department for education promised to  give children laptops for remote  learning all the way back in april many  still haven't got them according to  reports you apparently asked a meeting  this week  what have they been doing for the last  six months i mean it's a pretty good  question what's the answer  no that report was wrong and the what  they're doing right now  is distributing the the laptops  and i think that's important i think  there's over i'm told  there's over half a million laptops that  have been uh distributed  now this is a really important and it's  important that it's happening  right now um and i i think you know the  dfe have been working  incredibly hard in very difficult  circumstances  and difficult circumstances brought on  by the need to control the virus  and they i think that it's been  it of course it's a challenge in  education  when the rules and the approach changes  because the data changes this is the  thing and  and the sophie the frank truth is the  public uh completely get this  when there's a new variant of a virus  that is  um that is spreading fast then we have  to act  fast to control it i think the public  really understand that and then  and i know that the public would rather  that we acted uh  rather than uh thinking saying well  you know somebody in the media is going  to call it a u-turn that isn't  that is what matters what matters is  taking the right decisions  based on the latest data that you have  public think  um gavin williamson said that you know  parents with concerns about their school  should contact ofsted who should parents  contact  if they've got concerns about the  performance of gavin williamson  well i think gavin's been doing a  brilliant job in very difficult  circumstances  and it actually it's incumbent on all of  us  frankly the whole country to pull  together as much as possible  you know this is something that we can't  get through uh  by uh by by only some people acting  everybody has a role to play and yes of  course  yes of course we in government have a a  a critical  responsibility and i you know i feel  that  uh the the duty to get these decisions  right  i feel that very heavily but we've all  got it we've all got a responsibility  and i frankly i don't think um  politics comes into it what matters is  making the right substantive judgments  and trying to make those judgments on  the best latest available information so  if if politics doesn't come into it um  what do you think persuaded the prime  minister to appoint the  former chief whip who knows where all  the bodies are buried to the job of  education secretary  well i think gavin's been doing a great  job and i i think what matters is  people working you know working at all  of us working as a team  and but it's not just about government  that's the thing right we talk to  a huge range of people outside  government as well but it also comes  down to every single one of your viewers  this is a huge team effort and that's  that's how  it's the only way that we can get  through